msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:101
#: 979924a817fc42368162ca92ec9f8525
msgid "Reporting Security Issues"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:103
#: dcd16e2b42ec4dc298fd818b69261169
msgid "If you believe you have found a security issue in ArduPilot, please raise an issue in the `issues list <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/issues>`__ and optionally post a link to the issue in the :ref:`ArduPilot Discord Server's <ardupilot-discord-server>` \"code_review\" channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:105
#: b336ad3f81904feaac36c04465ef7e2d
msgid "While we are keen to hear of potential security issues, before reporting, please read the \"Security Attack Surface\" and \"Ground Control Station\" sections above.  The most common reports we receive are from `fuzz testing <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuzzing>`__ involving changing various parameters in SITL and causing the vehicle to crash or sending in invalid MAVLink messages.  These are generally not valid security issues because the ground station is considered trusted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:108
#: f6fcda6661f84565aaa30ef285da22bc
msgid "Common Misconceptions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:110
#: f6b16ff6aa064ebb9ac505d6d24a906b
msgid "*\"ArduPilot is open source, so it must be insecure\"*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:112
#: c9d24f307d1b41f6800335a1fb091e4d
msgid "Absolutely incorrect.  The \"open\" in \"open source\" refers to the fact that the source code is available for anyone to read which contributes to security by having more sympathetic eyes finding and reporting issues.  This does not mean that anyone can change it, nor does it mean that it must have \"open\" attack surfaces.  ArduPilot's open source software is \"compiled\" before use (as is all C++ software), once compiled there's no fundamental difference between open and closed source software.  Here are some supporting articles:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:114
#: c6e337b5b58242f69a92ef5b76a2313e
msgid "`efrontl's Open-source and the “security through obscurity” fallacy <https://www.efrontlearning.com/blog/2012/04/open-source-and-the-security-through-obscurity-fallacy.html>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:115
#: 7f668461be964a358db664c341698e08
msgid "`PingCap's Evaluating Security: Open Source vs Proprietary Software <https://www.pingcap.com/article/evaluating-security-open-source-vs-proprietary-software/>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:116
#: a9996088014447ca944010ddb1a48f6c
msgid "`LinuxFoundation's Combating Cyber Threats: The Power of Open Source <https://www.linuxfoundation.org/blog/combating-cyber-threats-the-power-of-open-source/>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:117
#: 9708f6a0df874fccb0d71e451eee7845
msgid "`SimpleRisk's The Security of Open Source vs Closed Source Software <https://www.simplerisk.com/blog/security-open-source-vs-closed-source-software>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:118
#: 9fd6a022b673423fb05b8f0e2aea5932
msgid "`WilsonCenter's Open Source Software and Cybersecurity: How unique is this problem? <https://www.wilsoncenter.org/blog-post/open-source-software-and-cybersecurity-how-unique-problem>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:119
#: 7a73d791fd8b4a8eabf3789d5d18081b
msgid "`Pingdom's 75 Percent of Top 10K Websites Served by Open Source Software <https://www.pingdom.com/blog/75-percent-top-10k-websites-served-by-open-source-software/>`__ (shows how open source can be safe)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:121
#: a7c95038902a493eb9243ceadb7dfd4f
msgid "*\"Open source software is lower quality than closed source software\"*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:123
#: 91b643a7d78044a6935360a3b1471589
msgid "Completely false.  Code quality is determined by the developers, the review process, the amount of testing and release procedures, not by whether it is open or closed source."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/security-landing-page.rst:125
#: 63dd3928c1ac449eb69578bd233195d4
msgid "The core ArduPilot development team is very focused on code quality and has a rigorous code review process.  Each change (aka Pull Request) must pass hundreds of automated checks and be reviewed by one or more dev team members before being \"merged\" into the main code base.  After merging the combined code is continuously subject to automated testing and real flight testing by the core dev team and community members before even reaching the beta testing stage.  Beta testing of major releases normally takes 3 months during which time partner companies and community members test the software on a wide range of vehicles.  Incident reports are logged, investigated and fixes applied as required.  Only after beta testing is complete is the software released as the stable/official version.  Post release, the core dev team carefully monitors the forums for incidents and prioritises bug fixes over enhancements, dutifully reporting critical issues in a timely manner to the community."
msgstr ""
